(* Q1: State and prove Lagrange's Mean Value Theorem and give its geometric) Tj
T*
(interpretation.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Statement: If f\(x\) is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on \(a, b\), there) Tj
T*
(exists at least one c in \(a, b\) such that f'\(c\) = [f\(b\) - f\(a\)] / \(b - a\). Proof: Define) Tj
T*
(auxiliary function phi\(x\) = f\(x\) - A*x, choosing constant A such that phi\(a\) = phi\(b\).) Tj
T*
(This yields A = [f\(b\) - f\(a\)] / \(b - a\). phi\(x\) satisfies all three conditions of) Tj
T*
(Rolle's Theorem on [a, b]. Hence, there exists c in \(a, b\) such that phi'\(c\) = 0. Since) Tj
T*
(phi'\(x\) = f'\(x\) - A, phi'\(c\) = 0 implies f'\(c\) = A = [f\(b\) - f\(a\)] / \(b - a\).) Tj
T*
(Geometrically, this means there is at least one point on the curve where the tangent) Tj
T*
(line is strictly parallel to the secant line joining endpoints \(a, f\(a\)\) and \(b, f\(b\)\).) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Derive the formula for the radius of curvature in polar coordinates r =) Tj
T*
(f\(theta\).) Tj
T*
(Answer: In polar coordinates, x = r*cos\(theta\) and y = r*sin\(theta\). Differentiating) Tj
T*
(with respect to theta yields dx/d\(theta\) = r'*cos - r*sin and dy/d\(theta\) = r'*sin +) Tj
T*
(r*cos. The arc differential ds^2 = dx^2 + dy^2 = \(r^2 + \(r'\)^2\)*d\(theta\)^2, giving) Tj
T*
(ds/d\(theta\) = sqrt\(r^2 + \(r'\)^2\). The angle psi made by the tangent with the polar axis) Tj
T*
(satisfies tan\(psi\) = \(dy/d\(theta\)\) / \(dx/d\(theta\)\). Differentiating psi with respect to) Tj
T*
(theta and using rho = ds / d\(psi\) = \(ds/d\(theta\)\) / \(d\(psi\)/d\(theta\)\) yields the) Tj
T*
(standard polar formula: rho = [ r^2 + \(r_1\)^2 ]^{3/2} / [ r^2 + 2*\(r_1\)^2 - r*r_2 ],) Tj
T*
(where r_1 = dr/d\(theta\) and r_2 = d^2r/d\(theta\)^2.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
